\section*{Executive Summary}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Executive Summary}

\textbf{Decision.} A telecom operator has a fixed retention budget and must decide, every campaign wave, \emph{which customers receive a retention offer and which offer they receive}. The current practice is to call the customers most likely to leave. This report asks whether adding each customer's economic value and the cost of an offer to the decision produces more value from the same budget, and how much should be spent.

\textbf{Approach.} Following CRISP-DM, we framed the decision first (unit: one customer; outcome: churn; decision: offer or not, and which offer, within a budget), then built a calibrated churn model on the IBM Telco Customer Churn sample (<< data.rows | num >> customers, << data.churn_rate | pct >> churners), a transparent customer-value proxy, and an expected-value (EV) decision rule
\[ \text{EV}_{i,o} \;=\; p_i \times q_o \times V_i \;-\; (c^{\text{offer}}_o + c^{\text{contact}}) \]
where $p_i$ is the predicted churn probability, $q_o$ the assumed \emph{save rate} of offer $o$, and $V_i$ the customer's discounted 12-month margin. An integer program chooses customers and offers within the budget. All choices were made on a validation split; the held-out test split (<< data.splits.test | num >> customers) was used once.

\textbf{Findings.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{The model ranks churners well and its probabilities can be trusted for money calculations:} calibrated logistic regression, ROC-AUC << model.selected_test.roc_auc | num(3) >>, PR-AUC << model.selected_test.average_precision | num(3) >> (random baseline << data.churn_rate | num(3) >>), expected calibration error << model.selected_test.ece | num(3) >>. Random forest and XGBoost did not rank better, so the simpler, explainable model is deployed.
  \item \textbf{Same budget, more value.} With << budget.base | usd >> on the test customers, \emph{<< strategy_label >>} yields an expected net value of << chosen.expected_net_value | usd >> (ROI << chosen.roi | pct(0) >>) against << c.risk.expected_net_value | usd >> for today's highest-risk rule: \textbf{<< headline.delta_vs_risk | signed_usd >>}, of which << headline.targeting_effect | usd >> comes from choosing customers by expected value and << headline.offer_design_effect | usd >> from matching offers to segments. Valued with customers' actual churn the gain is << headline.gain_label.label_based_simulated_net | usd >> (95\% bootstrap interval << headline.gain_label.lower_95 | usd >> to << headline.gain_label.upper_95 | usd >>).
  \item \textbf{Who gets an offer:} << profile.by['InternetService|Fiber optic'].list_share | pct(0) >> of the recommended list have fibre internet and << profile.by['Contract|Month-to-month'].list_share | pct(0) >> a month-to-month contract, with a median bill of << profile.list.median_monthly_charges | usd >> (<< profile.all.median_monthly_charges | usd >> overall). Calling the most \emph{valuable} customers loses money (<< c.value.expected_net_value | usd >>), and so does random contact (<< c.random_mean.expected_net_value | usd >>): risk and value are needed together.
  \item \textbf{How much to spend:} keeping only budget steps that still return at least << recommendation.hurdle_marginal_roi | pct(0) >> gives about << scaled.recommended_budget | usd >> for the whole base (<< budget.recommended_per_base_customer | usd(2) >> per customer); on the test customers this buys << recommended.selected_count | num >> offers for << recommended.cost | usd >> with expected net value << recommended.expected_net_value | usd >>.
  \item \textbf{Main risk:} the save rate is an assumption, not a measurement. The recommended list breaks even if the standard offer saves << rbe_std | pct >> of would-be leavers (assumed << q | pct(0) >>) and loses money in << mc.recommended.prob_loss | pct(0) >> of << monte_carlo.draws | num >> Monte Carlo draws of all assumptions; expected-value targeting nevertheless beats risk-only targeting in << mc.base_vs_risk.prob_gain | pct(2) >> of draws. \emph{Who} to target is robust; \emph{how much} an offer saves is not.
\end{itemize}

\keybox{hustblue}{\textbf{Recommendation.} Adopt expected-value targeting with segment-specific offers, and launch it as a \textbf{randomised 90-day pilot} of << pilot.list_size_per_wave | num >> customers (half held out as a control). Scale up only if the measured save rate of the offer mix is statistically significant and at least << rbe | pct >>, its break-even (assumed: << qmix | pct >>). The data contain no randomised campaign, so no causal uplift is claimed; all money figures are expected values under visible, documented assumptions in USD of the fictional sample company.}

