\textbf{Cleaning decisions.} The << data.quality.total_charges_filled_zero >> blank \texttt{TotalCharges} values all belong to customers with \texttt{tenure} = 0, i.e.\ customers who have not yet received a bill. The values are missing for a structural reason (not at random), so they were set to 0 instead of being imputed with a mean. No other rows were filtered. Remaining missing numeric inputs in uploaded files are imputed with \emph{training-split} medians inside the model pipeline, so no information from the validation or test customers leaks into training.

\subsection{Validation design}

The sample is a single cross-sectional snapshot with no dates, so a temporal hold-out is impossible. We therefore used a stratified random split into train / validation / test = << data.splits.train | num >> / << data.splits.validation | num >> / << data.splits.test | num >> customers (60/20/20, churn rate preserved in each part). Every choice (features, hyper-parameters, calibration, model, decision threshold, offer strategy, risk/value cut-offs and the budget) was made on train or validation; the test split was used once for reporting. Because each customer appears exactly once, there is no repeated-entity leakage between splits. With real operator data, the split should become temporal (train on earlier months, test on a later month); this is listed as a deployment requirement.

\subsection{Leakage audit}\label{sec:leakage}

Only information available at the scoring date may be used. Table~\ref{tab:leak} lists every field that was excluded and why.

