\section{From Prediction to Decision}

\subsection{Customer value proxy}

The data contain no margin or lifetime, so value is a transparent proxy built from tenure and charges, as the brief suggests:
\begin{gather*}
V_i \;=\; \text{MonthlyCharges}_i \times m \times a\!\left(H_i, r\right),\
H_i = \min\!\big(H_{\max},\, H_0 \cdot f_{\text{seg}(i)}\big),\\
a(H,r) = \frac{1-(1+r_m)^{-H}}{r_m},
\end{gather*}
with contribution margin $m$ = << cfg.value.margin | pct(0) >>, base horizon $H_0$ = << cfg.value.base_horizon_months >> months scaled by tenure segment ($f$ = << cfg.value.tenure_factors.new >> / << cfg.value.tenure_factors.developing >> / << cfg.value.tenure_factors.loyal >> for new / developing / loyal customers), cap $H_{\max}$ = << cfg.value.horizon_cap_months >> months and a << cfg.value.discount_rate_annual | pct(0) >> annual discount rate ($r_m$ is the equivalent monthly rate). It is a deliberately short, truncated value \cite{faderhardie2014,gupta2004}: a saved would-be churner is likely to leave again sooner than an average customer. As a sanity check, its rank correlation with IBM's CLTV index is only << cltv.spearman_value_proxy | num(2) >>, because that index is driven by tenure (<< cltv.spearman_tenure | num(2) >>) whereas our proxy measures the \emph{next} months of margin at stake.

\subsection{Expected value of an offer and the decision rule}

For customer $i$ and offer $o$:
\[
\text{EV}_{i,o} \;=\; \underbrace{p_i}_{\text{churn risk}} \times \underbrace{q_o}_{\text{save rate}} \times \underbrace{V_i}_{\text{value}} \;-\; \big(\underbrace{c^{\text{offer}}_o + c^{\text{contact}}}_{\text{cost}}\big).
\]
The offer cost is charged to every contacted customer (an unconditional credit), which is conservative. A customer is worth contacting only when $\text{EV} > 0$; equivalently, the customer's churn probability must exceed the \emph{break-even probability} $c/(q\,V_i)$, which depends on the customer's value. On test this break-even probability ranges from << be.break_even_probability_quantiles.p10 | pct(0) >> for the most valuable decile to above 100\% (never worth an offer) for the least valuable ones, which is why a single probability threshold cannot be right.

