\subsection{Randomised pilot}

Because the save rate cannot be estimated from these data, the recommendation is deployed as an experiment \cite{kohavi2020,ascarza2018}. One wave of << pilot.list_size_per_wave | num >> customers selected by the recommended rule is split at random: << pilot.arm_size_per_wave | num >> receive the offer and << pilot.arm_size_per_wave | num >> are held out. The save rate is the relative reduction of churn in the offered half against the held-out half after << pilot.outcome_window_days >> days. Because the offered half receives a mix of offers, this measures the mix's average save rate, assumed to be << qmix | pct >>; the list breaks even at << rbe | pct >> on this scale.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Power.} With << (pilot.alpha * 100) | num(0) >>\% significance and << (pilot.power * 100) | num(0) >>\% power, detecting the assumed << qmix | pct >> save rate needs << pilot.at_assumed.customers_per_arm | num >> customers per arm, and detecting the break-even rate (<< rbe | pct >>) needs << pilot.at_break_even.customers_per_arm | num >>; one wave of << pilot.arm_size_per_wave | num >> per arm covers both.
  \item \textbf{Decision rule, agreed in advance:} << pilot.decision_rule >>; at most << cfg.project.max_extend_waves >> extra waves. If the true save rate is the assumed << qmix | pct >>, the rule says ``go'' with probability << pilot.at_assumed.p_go | pct(0) >>.
  \item \textbf{Cost and downside:} offer and contact cost about << pilot.wave_cost | usd >> for the offered half, expected net value << pilot.wave_expected_net | usd >>, worst case (nobody saved) << (-pilot.wave_cost) | usd >>. A decision is available about << pilot.weeks_to_decision >> weeks after approval.
\end{itemize}

